# TODO - Universal asset importer

## Objective

Provide one shared import and conversion interface.

## Acceptance

- Identify supported original media and validate the input revision.
- Safely extract and normalise user-provided assets into local storage.
- Validate deterministic output without executing original binaries.
- Support one preferred official edition per title initially.

## Evidence

The shared importer verifies SHA-256 of an immutable source snapshot before
opening the ZIP. The flat-file adapter admits stored and deflated entries
through shared archive budgets, rejects duplicate and case-colliding names,
unsafe paths, unsupported member types and inconsistent ZIP headers. It
checks decoded length and CRC before each conversion callback. Six synthetic
tests pass. The read-only verification CLI also passed on the pinned
Simpsons World set 1 input with exact source, member, total and count limits.
It decoded 14 members and 7,209,088 bytes without extraction or execution.
Synthetic conversion tests cover deterministic manifest ordering, payload
deduplication, output budget enforcement and rejection after source or
staging errors. The staging sink is caller-owned and must discard all
outputs on failure. The Linux adapter verifies the exact content-addressed
payload set and stages the manifest in the same private directory. Native
tests exercise bundle integrity and rejection of missing, extra and wrong
content. Title-specific conversion and publication integration remain open.

GNU/Linux publication now synchronises each unique payload, the manifest
and their staging directories before an atomic no-replace rename into a
manifest-SHA-256-addressed sibling directory. The receipt distinguishes a
successful rename with failed parent-directory sync from a durable
publication. Synthetic tests verify persistence, exact manifest and payload
bytes, and rejection of existing destinations (including a symlink).
Fresh validation passed in run 1795578494631580142-2996234.
Crash fault injection, runtime consumption and Android publication remain
unverified. The publication parent and staging files must remain trusted.

The host composition tool now connects the selected graphics converter
to native transactional staging without introducing an importer-to-platform
dependency. On GNU/Linux the actual pinned Simpsons archive produced a
published manifest-addressed bundle with 10,485,760 payload bytes.
Independent pinned-manifest read-back verified both payloads.
A repeated import rejected the existing destination and left its contents
unchanged. Outputs are retained only beneath the ignored local reference
directory. Other titles, Android publication and full gameplay remain open.

The MAME adapter now selects an explicit machine and pinned revision. The
Simpsons machine is a declarative Konami board profile: the shared adapter
collects exact ROM lanes, reuses chip bit layouts, converts each region and
checks the expected manifest shape. The host CLI uses MAME verify, import,
publish and check operations rather than title-specific flags. Local native
round-trip verification reproduced the previously pinned manifest identity
and both payload hashes. The old bundle also passed the new reader.
Only the selected Simpsons profile is verified; other boards remain open.

The same machine selection and transactional publication path now support
a distinct canonical audio family. The KDSC catalogue parser checks every
header and contiguous sample extent before converting one clip at a time.
The pinned Simpsons source produced 161 mono PCM16 records, 3,798,888
payload bytes in total, with declared nominal rates preserved. Two imports
and native published read-back returned the same manifest; repeated
publication rejected the destination and read-back remained unchanged.
The graphics family retains its previous manifest identity. A cross-family
read-back was rejected. This verifies asset conversion, not game audio.
